<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Guangli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 88 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Guangli was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> general under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> and the brother of the emperor's favourite consort, Lady Li. He led the war against Dayuan in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana</a> valley, which after a failed first campaign forced the kingdom to submit and surrender its horses in 102 BCE. He later fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, surrendered to them in 90 BCE and was put to death among them.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Guangli belonged to the Li family favoured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a>. His sister Lady Li was the emperor's favourite concubine, which made him the emperor's brother-in-law, and his brother Li Yannian was also close to the emperor. When the crown prince Liu Ju took his own life in 91 BCE, Li Guangli's nephew Liu Bo was among the candidates to succeed him as heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Li was the general chosen for the War of the Heavenly Horses, fought to obtain the fine horses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana</a> that the Han had demanded and been refused. He was given the title General of Erh-shih in expectation of taking that city, which lay probably near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. The supplies for his second expedition are recorded as 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and 30,000 horses, with many mules and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>. He besieged Erh-shih, diverted the river that supplied its inner city with water, and received three thousand horses in tribute.</p><p>In 90 BCE, while he was campaigning in the north against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, his wife was imprisoned in the capital after a political scandal involving their relative by marriage Liu Qumao, one of whose sons had married a daughter of the Li. Hoping that a quick victory would win her release, Li pushed his army too far and was decisively defeated by a Xiongnu force of 50,000 under the Chanyu Hulugu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the defeat Li surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, and the Chanyu Hulugu gave him his own daughter in marriage. About a year later Li came into conflict with Wei Lü, another Han defector who stood high in the Chanyu's favour, and he was executed among the Xiongnu as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Li Guangli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 104 BCE Dayuan refused to supply horses, and Emperor Wu sent the general <b>Li Guangli</b>, who after two campaigns besieged Ershi in 102 BCE, forcing the nobles to kill their king Wugua and hand over horses.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dayuan">Kingdom of Dayuan</a></li></ul></section>
